{
 "result": "csvsum.py is built and verified: it reads a CSV with header 'id,cat,val' on stdin and prints each cat with the sum of val to 2 decimals, sorted by cat. It is a launcher that execs a compiled C helper, csvsum_c (built from csvsum.c with `cc -O2 -pthread`: buffer split into chunks at newlines, per-thread hash tables, sums kept as integer cents, then merged and sorted), with a pure-Python Decimal fallback (CSVSUM_PURE=1) if the helper cannot be built or run. Correctness evidence, re-checked on the final body after cleanup: on the generated 2,000,000-row data.csv (gen.py, 50 categories), output is byte-identical (cmp) to expected.txt, which was produced by an independent reference (ref.py, csv module + Decimal), for file stdin, piped stdin and the pure-Python mode; a small hand case gave the right sums (a 5.25, b 1.00), and earlier runs also passed an empty-body input and a 1000-category file where the single-threaded and multithreaded builds agreed. Speed evidence: the assay `python3 assay.py` (1 / best wall time of 7 runs, prints 0 on any output mismatch) was registered at 1.746 on the pure-Python version; the single-threaded C build measured 10.6\u201313.4 and the final multithreaded build measured 21.0, 19.7 and 22.4 on three runs (about 0.05 s per run including Python startup, roughly 12x the baseline). These later figures are my own runs of the assay script, not harness re-registrations, and the multithreading was applied directly rather than through a scored `try`. Untested ideas left: -O3/-march=native, cheaper hashing, and avoiding the Python launcher startup (~0.02 s of the ~0.05 s).",
 "calls": 20,
 "secs": 995.5
}
